content:
{
  "findings": [
    {
      "title": "Inclusion criterion 3b requires PD within 12 weeks of last dose, but criterion 3a requires treatment for 12 weeks or longer, creating a potential contradiction for patients who progress during treatment.",
      "page": 38,
      "quote": "a. Received treatment with a Food and Drug Administration approved PD-1 blocking antibody for 12 weeks or longer. b. Have PD (according to RECIST v1.1) within 12 weeks of the last dose of a PD-1 blocking antibody",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inclusion criterion 4c requires documented disease progression in a previously radiated lesion to serve as a target lesion, but this is not a standard RECIST v1.1 requirement and may be impossible if no such lesion exists.",
      "page": 38,
      "quote": "c. Documented disease progression in any lesion that was previously radiated in order to serve as a target lesion.",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inclusion criterion 5 allows archival sample if no intervening therapy, but this contradicts the requirement for refractory to PD-1 blockade (criterion 3) which implies prior therapy; archival sample may be from before that therapy.",
      "page": 38,
      "quote": "A fresh tissue biopsy (within 90 days before the start of study treatment) is preferred but an archival sample is acceptable (as a substitute) if no intervening therapy was received.",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inclusion criterion 7a lists white blood cells ≥ 2000/mm3, but this is not a standard bone marrow function criterion and may be inconsistent with other hematologic requirements.",
      "page": 39,
      "quote": "− white blood cells ≥ 2000/mm3",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Exclusion criterion 4 allows corticosteroids ≤ 10 mg/day, but Section 4.4 prohibits systemic pharmacologic doses > 10 mg/day, which is consistent, but the note in exclusion 6 requires diagnostic tests for subjects receiving > 5 mg and ≤ 10 mg for > 2 weeks, which may be overly cautious and not standard.",
      "page": 40,
      "quote": "NOTE: Subjects previously treated with a CTLA-4–blocking antibody, subjects receiving corticosteroids with daily doses > 5 mg and ≤ 10 mg of prednisone equivalent for > 2 weeks, and subjects with clinical symptoms and/or laboratory findings suggesting risk for adrenal insufficiency should undergo diagnostic tests for adrenal insufficiency via local laboratory.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Exclusion criterion 10 allows adjuvant hormonal therapy for breast cancer > 3 years from curative-intent surgical resection, but this is not a standard exception and may be inconsistent with the 3-year active treatment exclusion.",
      "page": 40,
      "quote": "and adjuvant hormonal therapy for breast cancer > 3 years from curative-intent surgical resection.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Exclusion criterion 18 prohibits blood products or colony stimulating factors within 30 days before start of Screening, but this is not a standard exclusion and may be overly restrictive.",
      "page": 41,
      "quote": "Received blood products (including platelets or red blood cells) or colony stimulating factors (including granulocyte colony stimulating factor, granulocyte/macrophage colony stimulating factor, or recombinant erythropoietin) within 30 days before the start of Screening.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Section 4.3.1 requires FSH > 40 mIU/mL on 2 measurements 2 months apart for females with less than 12 months amenorrhea, but this is not a standard criterion and may be impractical.",
      "page": 41,
      "quote": "Females with less than 12 months of amenorrhea must have a serum follicle stimulating hormone (FSH) level > 40 mIU/mL on 2 measurements performed 2 months apart",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Section 4.4 prohibits live/attenuated vaccines until 100 days post last dose, but Section 4.4.1 recommends avoiding viral vector or mRNA vaccines within 1 week of CMP-001 injection, which is inconsistent (live vs. non-live).",
      "page": 42,
      "quote": "Any live / attenuated vaccine (eg, varicella, zoster, yellow fever, rotavirus, oral polio and measles, mumps, rubella [MMR]) during treatment and until 100 days post last dose.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Section 4.5 states treatment discontinuation if subject becomes pregnant, but Section 4.3.2 requires contraception until 150 days after last dose, which is consistent, but the protocol does not specify how pregnancy is managed for the partner.",
      "page": 44,
      "quote": "Subject becomes pregnant or begins breastfeeding",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Section 5.1.1 states nivolumab 360 mg IV at W1D1 and Q3W thereafter, but Section 5.1.1.1 discusses 240 mg Q2W and 480 mg Q4W as approved, and the dose selection rationale does not explicitly justify 360 mg Q3W over these.",
      "page": 45,
      "quote": "Subjects will receive CMP-001 10 mg IT injection followed by nivolumab 360 mg IV at W1D1 and Q3W thereafter",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Section 5.1.2 states CMP-001 10 mg weekly for 7 doses (W1D1 to W7D1), but Section 5.1.2.2 mentions observation for first 6 injections (W1D1 to W6D1) and reduction from 7th (W7D1), which is consistent, but the protocol does not specify the observation period for subsequent injections after W7D1.",
      "page": 48,
      "quote": "Subjects must be observed for at least 4 hours following each of the first 6 CMP-001 injections (W1D1 to W6D1). Beginning with the 7th CMP-001 injection (W7D1), the observation period may be reduced to a minimum of 1 hour following CMP-001 injection at the Investigator’s discretion based on the AE profile of the individual subject.",
      "severity": "minor"
    }
  ]
}
